WEBVTT

00:00:00.011 --> 00:00:04.599
Hi, I'm Thushara and this is the records table

00:00:04.643 --> 00:00:09.108
in Gistly, where QA analysts and managers find the calls to audit.

00:00:09.503 --> 00:00:14.246
I redesigned the table and built the front end using coding agents

00:00:14.343 --> 00:00:19.038
and it went from this to

00:00:19.039 --> 00:00:22.480
this. And here is what I changed and why.

00:00:23.422 --> 00:00:25.526
When we looked at our Amplitude data,

00:00:25.527 --> 00:00:30.797
we found about half of our users were exporting the table to Excel.

00:00:30.798 --> 00:00:35.561
And the users told us the data looks very monotonous,

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.282
and the icons were difficult to understand,

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.906
and it was very hard to make sense of the data.

00:00:43.697 --> 00:00:47.565
If you look at this, you can see different data types are shown in

00:00:47.566 --> 00:00:52.613
a similar way, which makes it difficult because there's a lot of information

00:00:52.614 --> 00:00:57.191
here, and it might make it difficult for the user to make

00:00:57.192 --> 00:01:01.076
sense of it. And we also noticed that a lot of users were

00:01:01.077 --> 00:01:03.272
going back to a previous calls page,

00:01:03.273 --> 00:01:07.893
instead of using this new Records page where we had

00:01:07.894 --> 00:01:12.312
a lot of advanced features that is more useful for the user.

00:01:13.312 --> 00:01:15.807
When I started working on this redesign,

00:01:16.062 --> 00:01:20.081
the first thing I did was try to understand the workflow.

00:01:20.082 --> 00:01:23.636
So I exported the table to Excel,

00:01:23.637 --> 00:01:28.010
tried to understand how Excel is different from our platform,

00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:33.064
and I found out that there are more intuitive

00:01:33.065 --> 00:01:35.788
controls, and there is this

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:38.272
familiarity principle at play as well,

00:01:38.273 --> 00:01:42.883
because the managers and the QA analysts are so used to Excel.

00:01:43.499 --> 00:01:46.693
So we had to bridge that gap between,

00:01:46.703 --> 00:01:48.984
the existing workflows and our platform.

00:01:48.994 --> 00:01:51.035
First, I made the column headers

00:01:51.036 --> 00:01:53.400
clickable, where they can sort,

00:01:53.692 --> 00:01:58.502
filter, or hide, instead of just relying on this main filter option.

00:01:58.954 --> 00:02:03.834
I also added labels to these toolbar icons to make their functions

00:02:03.835 --> 00:02:06.483
clearer and easier to understand.

00:02:06.484 --> 00:02:09.442
Next, I focused on views.

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:13.154
One of the reviews

00:02:13.165 --> 00:02:17.976
we got was that the views are not understandable at all.

00:02:18.235 --> 00:02:20.843
So, I added this description,

00:02:21.113 --> 00:02:24.397
But rather than relying on a longer explanation,

00:02:24.398 --> 00:02:27.254
I wanted to make the feature immediately useful.

00:02:27.274 --> 00:02:30.782
So I introduced ready-made views such as Today,

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:33.420
This week, Flagged only, Follow-ups,

00:02:33.421 --> 00:02:36.976
and configured them on the backend.

00:02:37.593 --> 00:02:42.108
So, these were very common filtering patterns we identified

00:02:42.109 --> 00:02:46.822
from our user data. Now the user can quickly access different views

00:02:47.041 --> 00:02:51.228
they need without having to configure filters every time.

00:02:52.228 --> 00:02:56.135
I made sure that we use the color very intentionally because,

00:02:56.136 --> 00:03:00.660
it should communicate meaningful information rather

00:03:00.661 --> 00:03:05.743
than being just a decoration. I also introduced

00:03:05.744 --> 00:03:08.419
distinct styling for different data types,

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:10.887
user information, email address,

00:03:10.897 --> 00:03:16.152
phone numbers, and links, user and

00:03:16.153 --> 00:03:18.210
different other data types.

00:03:18.284 --> 00:03:22.985
So, it's much more distinct from each

00:03:22.986 --> 00:03:26.110
other. So, for the neutral data types,

00:03:26.111 --> 00:03:31.029
such as topics, I avoided the semantic colors so that the

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:35.378
styling wouldn't imply a level of importance that wasn't actually there.

00:03:35.389 --> 00:03:39.216
This was configured in the backend so that it is much more flexible.

00:03:39.748 --> 00:03:44.258
But the design tokens were very intentionally chosen, with

00:03:44.259 --> 00:03:49.027
accessibility in mind as well. When I worked on this using

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.271
the coding agents in the front-end,

00:03:51.272 --> 00:03:55.940
I was able to work on different minor things like

00:03:55.941 --> 00:03:59.745
the interactions that were very difficult

00:03:59.746 --> 00:04:02.041
to explore in Figma.

00:04:02.278 --> 00:04:07.441
So if you can see, previously we had this table where the stability

00:04:07.442 --> 00:04:11.113
was not that great. When you scroll up or down,

00:04:11.253 --> 00:04:13.575
the table was moving a lot.

00:04:14.563 --> 00:04:17.636
So here, when I was working on this, I was able to work,

00:04:17.637 --> 00:04:22.145
specifically on this table stability

00:04:22.146 --> 00:04:26.483
and work on this minor inconvenience. Interactions like the shadow,

00:04:26.494 --> 00:04:31.510
on scroll. I added this interaction

00:04:31.511 --> 00:04:36.701
where this sidebar folds, so there is more horizontal space.

00:04:36.712 --> 00:04:41.333
These changes made the experience feel more stable and gave users more room

00:04:41.334 --> 00:04:44.515
to work with their data. So, after the redesign,

00:04:44.516 --> 00:04:46.529
the share of active users,

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:50.238
using the Records page increased from 5% to 9%.

00:04:50.271 --> 00:04:55.190
And we also saw users shift towards

00:04:55.191 --> 00:04:59.619
saved views rather than manually configuring filters each time.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:02.891
The full write-up is available in the case study.

00:05:02.892 --> 00:05:04.046
Thank you for watching.
